    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2026-699",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Whether observed Atlantic Multidecadal variability (AMV) is truly an intrinsic internal mode of climate variability or an externally forced response remains contentious, with conflicting literature that North Atlantic SST variability arises from internal dynamics or external forcing. The availability of several single model initial-condition large ensembles (SMILEs) and new insights into potential biases in sea surface temperature (SST) variations offer a fresh opportunity to reassess this question. We show that SMILE ensembles provide strong evidence that AMV-like variability is largely externally forced. New insights into potential SST biases also raise questions about apparent early 20th Century oscillatory behaviour. SMILE models with stronger multidecadal variability show weaker agreement with observed AMV phasing, even in the best performing individual ensemble members, suggesting that large internal model variability may obscure the forced signal. We conclude that future variations in North Atlantic SST will very likely be driven primarily by future anthropogenic activities.",
    "field": "Climate variability",
    "problem": "The physical origin of Atlantic Multidecadal Variability remains contentious, with conflicting literature on whether it is an internal climate mode or a response to external forcing, a debate hindered by uncertain observational records and model limitations.",
    "method": "This study utilizes the ensemble mean from multiple large climate model ensembles (SMILEs and CMIP6) to isolate the externally forced signal and quantify its contribution to observed Atlantic Multidecadal Variability, thereby separating it from internal climate variability.",
    "task": "The task is to reassess whether observed Atlantic Multidecadal Variability (AMV) is an intrinsic internal mode of climate variability or an externally forced response, using single model initial-condition large ensembles to distinguish between these factors.",
